<!-- ─── §01: The Scale Argument ─────────────────── -->
<section id="s-scale" data-code="scale">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Scale Argument</div>
<h2>Inductive Biases Are Temporary Crutches</h2>
<p>
  Deep learning has a reliable pattern: methods that assume less tend to win as data grows. CNNs assumed spatial locality — transformers don't, and transformers won. Contrastive learning assumed negatives should be dissimilar — self-distillation doesn't, and self-distillation won. Modern SSL methods like DINO and iBOT still assume that a heavily augmented crop is "the same image" — but if the pattern holds, this assumption too will become a bottleneck.
</p>
<p>
  TDV (UIUC + NYU, including Yann LeCun) tests this directly. Using masking ratio as a proxy for assumption strength, they run the same SSL recipe at four data scales. The result is a clean crossover: at 0.1% of ImageNet, strong masking (50%) wins by 12+ percentage points; at 100% of ImageNet, weak masking (30%) surpasses it. The optimal assumption strength decreases with data.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">optimal inductive bias strength vs. data scale</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a data point to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:260px;">
    <canvas id="scale-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem;" class="scale-btns">
      <button class="scale-btn active" data-idx="0">0.1% data</button>
      <button class="scale-btn" data-idx="1">1% data</button>
      <button class="scale-btn" data-idx="2">10% data</button>
      <button class="scale-btn" data-idx="3">100% data</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The takeaway is uncomfortable: the biases that <em>enable</em> learning at low data are precisely the same biases that <em>restrict</em> it at scale. Adding SSL training data should improve performance — but strong augmentation constraints cap what can be learned. An assumption that is only approximately correct (e.g. "a cropped and color-jittered image is semantically identical") prevents the model from encoding information the assumption deems invariant.
</p>
<p>
  This motivates a search for a weaker, exact assumption. TDV's answer: causality — the past predicts the immediate future.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §02: Naive Removal Fails ─────────────────── -->
<section id="s-collapse" data-code="collapse">
<div class="section-no">02 — Naive Removal Fails</div>
<h2>You Can't Just Remove the Augmentations</h2>
<p>
  The obvious experiment: take DINO and progressively strip away its inductive biases. Remove color jitter, then local crops, then random global crop — and watch what happens. The results are stark. Every removal degrades KNN accuracy, and removing the final random crop entirely causes complete representation collapse: every image maps to the same embedding, giving 0.84% top-1 (near random).
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">progressive DINO ablation (SSv2 pretrain → ImageNet KNN top-1)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">bars animate on scroll</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="results-bars" id="collapse-bars">
      <p class="bar-group-label" style="margin-top:0">DINO baseline → removing inductive biases</p>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">2G+8L, full aug</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="cb0" style="background:var(--green)" data-w="24.63"><span>24.63%</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">− augmentations</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="cb1" style="background:rgba(107,185,129,.55)" data-w="16.44"><span>16.44%</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">− local crops</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="cb2" style="background:rgba(107,185,129,.4)" data-w="13.64"><span>13.64%</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">− crop on 1 global</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="cb3" style="background:rgba(107,185,129,.25)" data-w="8.04"><span>8.04%</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">− crop on both globals</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="cb4" style="background:var(--red,#ef4444)" data-w="0.84"><span>0.84% ✗ collapse</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="bar-group-label">TDV — no augmentations, no collapse</p>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <div class="bar-label">Full TDV recipe</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="cb5" style="background:var(--purple)" data-w="8.79"><span>8.79% ✓</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  TDV achieves 8.79% top-1 KNN — comparable to DINO without augmentations — while never collapsing. The key: it doesn't remove the learning signal entirely. Instead of enforcing invariance across views, it provides a <em>different</em> learning signal — the causal prediction of the next frame's embedding.
</p>
<p>
  Without some constraint, all representations collapse to a constant (trivially satisfying any loss). TDV's constraint is: <em>given the current frame, predict the next one</em>. This is exactly correct (the future is always caused by the past), never approximate, and prevents collapse by requiring the encoder to discriminate between different frames.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §03: TDV Architecture ─────────────────── -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — TDV Architecture</div>
<h2>Addition in Latent Space</h2>
<p>
  TDV's core principle is an additive decomposition in embedding space: the representation of a frame, when added to an encoded motion vector, should equal the representation of the next frame. No vision tricks. Just one equation:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">core TDV equation</div>
  <span class="math-var">ẑ</span><sub>t+1</sub> = <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub> + Δ<span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub><br>
  where <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">f</span><sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>), &nbsp; Δ<span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub> = <span class="math-var">m</span><sub>ϕ</sub>(Δ<span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>; <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub>)<br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Δx_t = x_&#123;t+1&#125; − x_t  (raw pixel difference between frames)</span>
</div>

<p>
  The <strong>frame encoder</strong> fθ maps each frame to a sequence of patch embeddings (n patches + [CLS] token). The <strong>motion encoder</strong> mϕ takes the raw pixel difference Δxₜ and, conditioned on the current frame embedding via cross-attention, produces a latent motion shift Δzₜ. Their sum is the predicted next-frame embedding.
</p>

