<!-- ─── §04: Low-Rank Motion ─────────────────── -->
<section id="s-motion" data-code="motion">
<div class="section-no">04 — Low-Rank Motion</div>
<h2>Why Consecutive Frames Are the Perfect Signal</h2>
<p>
  The motion encoder's job is to turn a pixel-level frame delta Δxₜ into a latent shift Δzₜ. Why does this work? Because the raw pixel difference between consecutive video frames is intrinsically <em>low-rank</em>: only the pixels belonging to moving objects change. The background — often 80–95% of pixels — contributes zero signal.
</p>
<p>
  This low-rank structure forces the motion encoder to capture <em>semantic</em> motion rather than raw pixel statistics. And because the same pixel-level change (say, a horizontal shift) can mean different things in different scenes — a sliding door vs. a running animal vs. a camera pan — the motion encoder is conditioned on the current frame's embedding zₜ via cross-attention.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">pixel delta is sparse — only moving regions carry signal</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">press Play to animate frame sequence</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:260px;">
    <canvas id="delta-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div class="delta-controls">
      <button id="delta-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button id="delta-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="delta-frame-label" id="delta-label">Frame 0 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  This is directly analogous to classical video codecs like MPEG and H.264: they store a keyframe plus compact inter-frame deltas (motion vectors). TDV is a learned, latent-space version of the same idea — except the "motion vectors" are abstract latent shifts rather than pixel-level block displacements, and they capture semantic rather than photometric change.
</p>

<h3>Why Not Just Use Temporal Invariance?</h3>
<p>
  One might wonder: can't you just train DINO with two consecutive video frames as the two views — treating them as an "augmentation"? The ablation table says no (row "No Motion Encoder" → 1.87% KNN, collapse). Simply declaring consecutive frames invariant discards the motion signal entirely, preventing the model from learning <em>what changed between frames</em>. TDV's insight is to model the difference explicitly, not ignore it.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §05: Results ─────────────────── -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">05 — Results</div>
<h2>Dense Spatial Tasks: TDV Wins Where It Counts</h2>
<p>
  TDV is pretrained on SomethingSomethingV2 (SSV2), a standard video benchmark with well-defined motion. Evaluation uses the pretrained backbone frozen, with task-specific heads for each downstream task. The comparison is against DINO and iBOT at ViT-S and ViT-B, pretrained on the same SSV2 data.
</p>
<p>
  On <strong>semantic segmentation</strong> (ADE20K, UperNet), TDV is competitive — 10.54 mIoU vs. DINO's 10.71 and iBOT's 10.60, a &lt;2% gap. On <strong>optical flow</strong> and <strong>stereo depth</strong> — the spatial tasks that require understanding <em>how pixels correspond across frames</em> — TDV wins clearly.
</p>

