<!-- ─── §06: Implications ─────────────────── -->
<section id="s-future" data-code="future">
<div class="section-no">06 — Implications</div>
<h2>The Path to Scale</h2>
<p>
  TDV's most important property is not what it achieves today, but what it removes from the recipe. Every eliminated inductive bias is one less bottleneck as data and compute grow. The paper demonstrates the trend empirically — weaker assumptions win at scale — and TDV removes all vision-specific assumptions.
</p>

<h3>Cross-Modality Transfer</h3>
<p>
  TDV uses no vision-specific techniques. The only requirement is a modality with temporal consistency — consecutive samples should differ primarily in a low-rank way. Audio spectrograms, robot proprioception, tactile sensors: any temporal signal where the "background" changes slowly and "events" are sparse will support the same additive latent composition principle.
</p>

<h3>Efficient Video Encoding</h3>
<p>
  Standard video models run every frame through a full image encoder — O(T × N) operations for T frames and N patches. TDV opens a different path: encode a keyframe fully with fθ, then propagate subsequent frames with the lightweight motion encoder. Only the initial frame requires the heavy backbone; subsequent frames need only the compact motion Δz. This mirrors MPEG's keyframe + P-frame structure, now learned end-to-end in representation space.
</p>

<h3>The Remaining Gap</h3>
<p>
  TDV's semantic performance (KNN, linear probe) lags behind DINO. The paper attributes this to the absence of local/global crop diversity that DINO uses to build invariance to viewpoint, color, and scale. Future work could add minimal semantic supervision — perhaps a small number of category-level labels at scale — without reintroducing the per-sample augmentation pipeline.
</p>
<p>
  The deeper question TDV raises: if causality (the weakest temporal assumption) already gets within a few points of methods with strong semantic inductive biases, what does this suggest about where the semantic performance comes from? Potentially much of what SSL methods "learn" from augmentation invariance is recoverable from temporal structure alone — with larger video datasets and better hyperparameters to find out.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Vision Without Augmentations — TDV</h2>
    <p>TDV, or Temporal Difference in Vision, is a self-supervised visual representation learning method from UIUC and NYU (including Yann LeCun) that removes all strong inductive biases from visual SSL. Instead of augmentations, masking, or cropping, TDV uses one principle: the current frame's embedding plus an encoded motion vector should equal the next frame's embedding. This is implemented as a frame encoder and a motion encoder trained jointly, with a teacher-student EMA setup preventing collapse.</p>
    <p>On dense spatial tasks, TDV outperforms DINO and iBOT: optical flow endpoint error drops from 13.03 (DINO) to 9.84 (TDV) on MPI-Sintel, and stereo depth bad-pixel rate drops from 65.51% (iBOT) to 56.89% (TDV). Semantic segmentation is competitive at 10.54 vs 10.71 mIoU. The paper also confirms empirically that optimal inductive bias strength decreases as data scale increases — motivating a future where TDV-style assumptions-free learning becomes the dominant paradigm.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：无需数据增强的视觉表征学习——TDV</h2>
    <p>TDV（视觉时间差分）是来自UIUC和纽约大学（包括Yann LeCun）的自监督视觉表征学习方法，完全去除了传统视觉SSL方法中的强归纳偏置。它不依赖图像增强、遮蔽或裁剪，而只使用一个原则：当前帧的嵌入加上编码后的运动向量等于下一帧的嵌入。该方法通过帧编码器和运动编码器联合训练实现，使用教师-学生EMA框架防止表征崩溃。</p>
    <p>在密集空间任务上，TDV超越了DINO和iBOT：在MPI-Sintel数据集上，光流端点误差从DINO的13.03降至9.84；立体深度坏像素率从iBOT的65.51%降至56.89%。语义分割结果相当，mIoU为10.54对比DINO的10.71。论文还通过实验证实，随着数据规模增大，最优归纳偏置强度会下降，这为未来无假设学习范式成为主流提供了依据。</p>
